= Solution
{parent=2}

**False.** Take $\alpha=1$ and $\beta=\omega$. By the limit clause for <ordinal addition>,
$$
1+\omega=\sup_{n<\omega}(1+n)=\omega,
\qquad \omega+1>\omega.
$$
The first <order type> is one point followed by a countably infinite <sequence>, which is still of type $\omega$. The second has a greatest element after that infinite <sequence>, so its <order type> is the <successor ordinal> $\omega+1$. Thus <ordinal addition> is not commutative, even though the two resulting <sets> have equal <cardinality>.

= 3
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=3}

**False.** Use $\alpha=2$ and $\beta=\omega$. The recursive definition of <ordinal multiplication> gives
$$
2\omega=\sup_{n<\omega}2n=\omega,
\qquad \omega\cdot2=\omega+\omega>\omega.
$$
The first product concatenates countably many finite two-point blocks. The second concatenates two whole copies of the <order type> $\omega$, producing a strictly larger <ordinal>. Hence <ordinal multiplication> is not commutative.

= 4
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=4}

**True: <ordinal multiplication> on the left distributes over <ordinal addition>.** If $\alpha=0$ both sides are zero. Otherwise fix $\alpha>0$ and $\beta$, and induct on $\gamma$. At zero the identity follows from $\alpha0=0$. At a successor, use the recursive <ordinal multiplication> rule and the <associativity> just proved:
$$
\alpha(\beta+(\gamma+1))
=\alpha((\beta+\gamma)+1)
=\alpha(\beta+\gamma)+\alpha
=(\alpha\beta+\alpha\gamma)+\alpha
=\alpha\beta+\alpha(\gamma+1).
$$
For a nonzero <limit ordinal> $\lambda$, the <ordinals> $\beta+\xi$ are <cofinal> in $\beta+\lambda$. <Continuity> of <ordinal multiplication> in its right argument gives
$$
\alpha(\beta+\lambda)
=\sup_{\xi<\lambda}\alpha(\beta+\xi)
=\sup_{\xi<\lambda}(\alpha\beta+\alpha\xi)
=\alpha\beta+\alpha\lambda.
$$
For the last <equality>, $\alpha\xi$ is increasing and <cofinal> in $\alpha\lambda$, which is a <limit ordinal> because $\alpha>0$, and <ordinal addition> is continuous in its right argument. This establishes the limit step and the identity for all <ordinals>.

