= Solution
{parent=3}

**This encoding works.** Use three distinct tags $0,1,2$ and the <ordered pair> construction already proved <injective>. In the <set>
$$
\{\langle0,a\rangle,\langle1,b\rangle,\langle2,c\rangle\},
$$
exactly one member has first coordinate $0$, exactly one has first coordinate $1$, and exactly one has first coordinate $2$. <Equality> of two such <sets> therefore matches the members carrying each tag. <Injectivity> of the <ordered pair> construction then gives <equality> of $a$, of $b$ and of $c$, respectively.

This is a <tagged encoding of a finite tuple>. It remains valid when coordinates coincide with one another or with tags: the <equality> criterion for <ordered pairs> still distinguishes their first coordinates. <Finite set> formation using pairing and <union> makes the encoding a <set>.

= 4
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=4}

**This encoding works**, interpreting the outer three-coordinate brackets by the nested <ordered pair> convention established in item 1. <Equality> of the outer <ordered triples> gives
$$
\{0,a\}=\{0,a'\},\qquad \{1,b\}=\{1,b'\},\qquad \{2,c\}=\{2,c'\}.
$$
For any fixed <set> $k$, the map $x\mapsto\{k,x\}$ is <injective>. If $x=k$, <equality> $\{k,x\}=\{k,y\}$ makes the right-hand side a <singleton> and forces $y=k$. If $x\ne k$, membership of $x$ in $\{k,y\}$ forces $x=y$. Applying this argument with $k=0,1,2$ recovers the respective coordinates.

The outer brackets must encode an <ordered triple>, rather than an unordered <set> of three doubletons. The source uses three-coordinate angle brackets here; using the successful convention of item 1 makes their meaning explicit and supplies a genuine construction.

